# pdcm-card-tricks.ti59  PDCM, a card-swapping operating system for the TI-59.
# Horst Umlauf, "Karten-Tricks", CHIP 8/1979 pp. 40-44 (listing: Bild 9);
# the author's corrections, in reply to readers: CHIP 10/1979 p. 12.
# A step-by-step guide for TI-59 users: PDCM.md, next to this file.
#
# PDCM manages the magnetic cards for a program. The program asks for a data
# register or a program strip by number; PDCM shows the card it needs in Eng
# format, stops for R/S, checks the card by the number kept on it and reads it
# again if it is the wrong one. With 29 registers per data card, the memory is
# limited only by the stack of cards.
#
#   n SBR 480     CSTO  load the data card of C-memory n; R31 = its register
#   x SBR 508     CCRD  load data card x; R00 holds the number of a data card
#   s.nnn GTO 566 CGTO  go to step s of strip .nnn
#   s.nnn SBR 567 CSBR  call step s of strip .nnn, come back to the caller's strip
#   -.nnn SBR 610 CSUB  load the block 2 strip -.nnn
#   .001 SBR 567        shut down: save the state to card -2
#
# A card's number reads in Eng as 12.-03 (= .012). The display is in Eng only
# while PDCM waits for R/S. Flag 8 before CCRD: don't write the old data card
# back. Flag 8 set by hand while PDCM shows the new card: don't read it, start
# with an empty card. Registers 00 and 30-32 are PDCM's, and HIR 7/HIR 8 share the
# printer's Op 03/Op 04 zones.
#
# Strips of the article (corrected by the author; the caption of Bild 9 is wrong):
#   11.-03    listing a, steps 000-239, bank 1: start and generation
#   -998.-03  listing a, steps 240-435, bank 2
#   1.-03     listing b, bank 1: shutdown
#   -999.-03  listing c, bank 3: the routines
# Differences from the article: RST needs an R/S to start; the text prints as
# "GOOD-BYE FROM PDCM" and "ST FLG 8"; "Stelle 247" in correction 7d is
# probably step 427, where 19.011 would need two more steps than .011.
#
# The demo is not from the article; readers asked for one in CHIP 10/1979.
# Strip 12.-03 (A) writes n squared to C-memories 20, 40 and 60, on data cards
# 0, 1 and 2. It goes to strip 13.-03 (CGTO), which reads them back and prints
# them, calls DONE on strip 12.-03 (CSBR) and shuts the system down.
#
# By hand on a TI-59:
#   4 2nd Op 17  CLR         partition 639.39
#   read 11.-03  RST  R/S    shows -999.-03
#   2nd St flg 7             generation: first use of the system
#   R/S, read -999.-03       shows -998.-03
#   R/S, read -998.-03       shows -1. 00
#   2nd St flg 8  R/S        new card -1; now every register and flag is asked
#   R/S (13 times) until 7 HIR is printed
#   .012 R/S                 HIR 7: the next strip
#   R/S (8 times)            prints DATA AREA NOT EMPTY, shows 0.1
#   R/S                      shows -1. 00: write it to a blank card
#   R/S                      shows 0. 00: data card 0
#   2nd St flg 8  R/S        prints O.K, shows 12.-03
#   R/S, read 12.-03         A starts the demo; follow the cards in KEYSTROKES

MODEL: TI-59
PRINTER: on
PARTITION: 639.39

